    "question": {
      ":question/id": "10a188f5-6743-55a2-a159-61d684bbb55a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)",
